      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO CONDITIONAL SENTENCES"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Zero Conditional expresses general facts and universal truths using Simple Present tense in both clauses.",
        "First Conditional denotes real future possibilities using Simple Present in the conditional clause and Future Simple in the main clause.",
        "Second Conditional represents hypothetical or imaginary present/future situations using Simple Past (or 'were') and 'would + V1'.",
        "Third Conditional addresses past unreal conditions and regrets using Past Perfect ('had + V3') and 'would have + V3'.",
        "The conjunction 'unless' means 'if not' and cannot take an additional negative word in its conditional clause."
      ],
      "id": "7yl4mhc"
    },
